# Copy to .env and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file carries names and
# explanations, never values.
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env
#
# Bun loads apps/web/.env automatically for `bun run dev`, `gen:cms`, `seed` and
# the e2e suite, so one file covers every command. Full rationale for each
# variable: docs/decisions/0013-deployment-configuration.md.

# ── Required in production, optional locally ────────────────────────────────────
# Signs Payload sessions. payload.config.ts falls back to 'dev-secret-change-me'
# when NODE_ENV is not production, and THROWS when it is — so a production build
# fails rather than sign sessions with a value committed to a public repository.
# That failure is deliberate; do not work around it.
#
# Generate one per environment (never reuse across local/CI/production):
#   openssl rand -base64 32
PAYLOAD_SECRET=

# ── The one that fails silently ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults to file:./payload.db, which is correct locally and CANNOT work on a
# serverless host: the filesystem is read-only apart from a per-invocation /tmp,
# so an editor's save either errors or vanishes with the invocation — while the
# build stays green, because nothing in the build writes to the database.
#
# Leave blank for local development. For any deployment, point it at a hosted
# libSQL database (Turso or equivalent — the same @payloadcms/db-sqlite adapter):
#   DATABASE_URI=libsql://<your-db>.turso.io
DATABASE_URI=

# Required with a remote DATABASE_URI, ignored with a local file: path. Without
# it a remote URL cannot authenticate.
DATABASE_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ── Local e2e and seeding ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The editor account `bun run seed` creates and e2e/cms-round-trip.spec.ts signs
# in as. That test FAILS rather than skips when these are absent, deliberately: a
# round-trip proof that silently stops running is worse than not having one.
#
# Not secrets. The database they apply to is created by the seed and thrown away,
# so in CI they are repository *variables* rather than secrets — a masked value
# would only make a failed login harder to read in the log.
E2E_USER_EMAIL=e2e@example.com
E2E_USER_PASSWORD=e2e-local-password

# ── Optional, local convenience ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lets a second, throwaway `next dev` build into its own directory instead of
# lock-contending with the main dev server's .next/. Leave unset normally.
# NEXT_DIST_DIR=
